WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:04.800
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute&nbsp;
Papers with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.800 --> 00:00:12.000
Today we will try to create a copy of&nbsp;
ourselves, and place it into a virtual world.

00:00:12.560 --> 00:00:19.440
Now this will be quite a challenge. Normally,&nbsp;
to do this, we have to ask an artist to create&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.440 --> 00:00:28.880
a digital copy of us, which takes a lot of time&nbsp;
and effort. But, there may be a way out. Look.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.880 --> 00:00:36.080
With this earlier AI-based technique, we can take&nbsp;
a piece of target geometry, and have an algorithm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.080 --> 00:00:44.160
try to rebuild it to be used within a virtual&nbsp;
world. The process is truly a sight to behold.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.160 --> 00:00:50.720
Look at how beautifully it sculpts this piece of&nbsp;
geometry until it looks like our target shape!

00:00:51.600 --> 00:00:58.720
This is wonderful, but wait a second. If&nbsp;
we wish to create a copy of ourselves,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.720 --> 00:01:05.760
we probably want it to move around too. This&nbsp;
is, however, a stationary piece of geometry.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.320 --> 00:01:11.840
No movement is allowed here. So,&nbsp;
what do we do? What about movement?

00:01:12.800 --> 00:01:17.840
Well, have a look at this new technique,&nbsp;
where getting a piece of geometry&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.840 --> 00:01:25.440
with movement cannot get any simpler than this&nbsp;
- just do your thing, record it with a camera,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.440 --> 00:01:31.920
and give it to the AI. And, I have&nbsp;
to say that I am a little skeptical,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.640 --> 00:01:39.120
look, this is what a previous technique could get&nbsp;
us. This is not too close to what we are looking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.120 --> 00:01:49.600
for. So, let’s see what the new method can do&nbsp;
with this data. And! Uh-oh. This is not great. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.160 --> 00:01:58.160
is this it? Is the geometry cloning dream dead?&nbsp;
Well, don’t despair quite yet! This issue happens&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.160 --> 00:02:04.480
because our starting position and orientation&nbsp;
is not known to the algorithm, but,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.480 --> 00:02:12.320
it can be remedied. How? Well, by adding&nbsp;
additional data to the AI to learn from.

00:02:12.320 --> 00:02:17.120
And now, hold on to your papers,&nbsp;
and let’s see what it can do now!&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.840 --> 00:02:26.160
And…oh my goodness. Are you seeing what I am&nbsp;
seeing? Our movement is now replicated in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.160 --> 00:02:33.200
virtual world almost perfectly! Look at that&nbsp;
beautiful animation. Absolutely incredible.

00:02:33.840 --> 00:02:43.040
And, if even this is not good enough, look&nbsp;
at this result too. So good! Loving it.&nbsp;

00:02:43.680 --> 00:02:49.600
And, believe it or not, it has even more coolness&nbsp;
up the sleeve. If you have been holding on to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.600 --> 00:02:56.800
your papers so far, now, squeeze that paper,&nbsp;
because here comes my favorite part of this work.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.360 --> 00:03:03.040
And that is a step that the authors&nbsp;
call scene fitting. What is that?&nbsp;

00:03:03.680 --> 00:03:10.640
Essentially, what happens is that the AI&nbsp;
reimagines us as a video game character, and sees&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.640 --> 00:03:18.240
our movement, but does not have an idea as to what&nbsp;
our surroundings look like. What it does is that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.240 --> 00:03:26.320
from this video data, it tries to reconstruct our&nbsp;
environment, essentially, recreating it as a video&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.320 --> 00:03:35.600
game level. And that is quite a challenge. Look,&nbsp;
at first, it is not close at all. But, over time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.600 --> 00:03:41.760
it learns what the first obstacle should look&nbsp;
like, but still, the rest of the level...not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.760 --> 00:03:49.040
so much! Can this still be improved? Let’s have&nbsp;
a look together. As we give it some more time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.040 --> 00:03:54.880
and our character a few more concussions,&nbsp;
it starts to get a better feel of the level.

00:03:55.440 --> 00:04:01.760
And it really works for a variety of&nbsp;
difficult, dynamic motion types. Cartwheels,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.320 --> 00:04:09.680
backflips, parkour jumps, dance moves, you name&nbsp;
it. It is a robust technique that can do it all.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.640 --> 00:04:16.960
So cool! And note that the authors of the&nbsp;
paper gave us not just the blueprints for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.960 --> 00:04:23.040
the technique in the form of a research paper,&nbsp;
but they also provide the source code of this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.040 --> 00:04:31.200
technique to all of us, free of charge. Thank&nbsp;
you so much! I am sure this will be a huge help&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.200 --> 00:04:37.040
in democratizing the creation of video&nbsp;
games and all kinds of virtual characters.

00:04:37.600 --> 00:04:45.280
And, if we add up all of these together. We&nbsp;
get this. This truly is a sight to behold.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.000 --> 00:04:53.840
Look! So much improvement just one more paper down&nbsp;
the line. And just imagine what we will be able to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.840 --> 00:05:00.480
do a couple more papers down the line. Well, what&nbsp;
do you think? Let me know in the comments below!

00:05:53.120 --> 00:05:57.360
Thanks for watching and for your generous&nbsp;
support, and I'll see you next time!

